The document's front-matter declarations — the `<meta>` block and the fields an author writes inside it.

<section #sh-meta | `<meta>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *meta*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<meta>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<meta>`

<code #code:sh-meta-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>

<section | Introduction>
The paper begins.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-meta-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>

<section | Introduction>
The paper begins.
</minipage>

*Author writes &lt;meta type=article&gt; at the top with no &lt;article&gt; wrapper. The structural plugin reads type=article and generates: - the &lt;article&gt; container - &lt;article-front&gt; wrapping the original &lt;meta&gt; - &lt;article-body&gt; wrapping the section content &lt;title&gt; is promoted to &lt;article-title&gt; as the first child of &lt;meta&gt;. &lt;meta&gt; itself survives in the output, inside &lt;article-front&gt;.*

<code #code:sh-meta-2>
<meta type=book>
  <title | A Natural History of Elephants>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
</meta>

<chapter | Origins>
Content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-meta-2>
<meta type=book>
  <title | A Natural History of Elephants>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
</meta>

<chapter | Origins>
Content.
</minipage>

*type=book generates the book-shaped wrapper instead. Changing the single kwarg switches the entire output structure. Each book-part contains its own &lt;meta&gt; with &lt;book-part-title&gt;; no &lt;book-part-meta&gt; wrapper.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `article` `book` `book-part` `website` | Declares the document class. Resolved ONCE before structuring by enscribeDocTypeResolve: the value is validated against this set, stored on file.data, and an explicitly-set unknown type (a typo, an unbuilt class) is reported with a non-fatal diagnostic and falls back to "article" (always-renders). The structural plugins (enscribeArticleStructuring / enscribeBookStructuring) read the resolved class to decide which Layer 1 wrapper to generate around the document: type=article → <article> with <article-front>/<article-body>/<article-back>; type=book → <book> with <book-front>/<book-body>/<book-back>; type=book-part → <book-part> containing <meta> and body content directly (no nested front/body/back wrappers). type=website → no Layer 1 wrapper; enscribeWebsiteStructuring builds a nav model on file.data from the master's <nav> (#246). HTML render only — no JATS/BITS (a site is not a scholarly document). Default is "article" — the most common case. An absent type kwarg is the normal article case: silent default, no diagnostic. |
| `book-part-type` | kwarg | `chapter` `part` `introduction` `conclusion` `other` `preface` `foreword` `dedication` `appendix` `glossary` `colophon` `afterword` | For a single-file book-part (type=book-part) only: sets the book-part-type on the generated `<book-part>` wrapper (#176). This is how a standalone book-part file declares whether it is an appendix, preface, etc. — a single-file appendix needs book-part-type=appendix (#100), so it is not always "chapter". Read by enscribeBookStructuring; allowlisted but not lifted (a structural kwarg, like type — it configures the wrapper, it is not a descriptive `<meta>` field). Default "chapter" when unset. An unknown value is reported with a non-fatal diagnostic and the document still renders (always-renders). Ignored on type=article / type=book documents, which route their book-parts by the `<chapter>` / `<appendix>` / … shorthand instead. |
| `icon` | kwarg |  | Optional brand icon for a website's top bar (#246): the path or URL of the site icon, read by the website chrome (S2). Descriptive metadata, like the title; ignored by article/book documents. The brand NAME is `<meta>`'s title — there is no in-header `<icon>`/`<title>` tag. |

</section>

<section #sh-config | `<config>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *config*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<config>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<config>`

<code #code:sh-config-1>
<config citation-style=author-year number-sections=true />
</code>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*The authoring form for &lt;config&gt; is kwargs. Settings are read at the discovery pass into the configuration registry; the element itself produces no body output (it renders as an empty &lt;config&gt;).*

<code #code:sh-config-2>
<config number-figures=true number-tables=true show-source=true />
</code>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*More operational options, all from the live kwarg allowlist (citation-style, number-sections, number-figures, number-tables, number-equations, show-source, parse-data-tables, ref-prefix-).*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `notes` | kwarg |  |  |

</section>

<section #sh-title | `<title>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *title*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<title>`.

The document (or section) title, authored as a child of `<meta>`. One authored `<title>` resolves to the right Layer 1 element by context: inside an article it is promoted to `<article-title>`, inside a book to `<book-title>`, inside a section's `<meta>` to the matching section-title. You write `<title>`; the compiler picks the variant.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<title>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-title-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source (it carries document metadata, not body content). Titles may contain inline formatting.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

`<title>` takes no attributes. Its content is inline prose. Pair it with `<subtitle>` for a secondary line.

</section>

<section #sh-subtitle | `<subtitle>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *subtitle*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<subtitle>`.

A secondary title line, authored as a child of `<meta>` alongside `<title>`. Like `<title>`, it resolves to the contextual variant — `<article-subtitle>`, `<book-subtitle>`, or the matching section-subtitle.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<subtitle>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-subtitle-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | On the Convergence of Iterative Maps>
  <subtitle | A Fixed-Point Perspective>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

`<subtitle>` takes no attributes. Its content is inline prose.

</section>

<section #sh-abstract | `<abstract>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *abstract*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<abstract>`.

The document abstract, authored as a child of `<meta>`. Its content is block prose (one or more paragraphs); a structured abstract uses markdown bold for its sub-headings.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<abstract>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-abstract-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <abstract |
  Elephant populations shape vegetation and carbon storage, and so
  affect regional climate. We quantify the effect across 50 sites.
  >
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `unstructured` `structured` `graphical` `executive-summary` `other` | Distinguishes abstract types. A structured abstract has explicit sub-headings (Background, Methods, Results, Conclusion); the default is unstructured flowing prose. |
| `word-limit` | kwarg |  | Optional documentation of the journal's word limit. Informational only — it does not enforce. |

</section>

<section #sh-keywords | `<keywords>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *keywords*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<keywords>` (JATS `<kwd-group>`).

The document's keyword set, authored as a child of `<meta>`. Author the terms as the content; they are the discovery keywords a journal or index lists.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<keywords>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-keywords-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <keywords | elephants; carbon storage; regional climate; conservation>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

`<keywords>` takes no attributes. Its content is the keyword list as inline text.

</section>

<section #sh-subject | `<subject>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *subject*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<subject>`.

A subject classification for the document — a discipline, category, or subject-scheme term — authored as a child of `<meta>`. Repeat it for multiple classifications.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<subject>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-subject-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <subject | Ecology>
  <subject scheme=msc | 92D40>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `scheme` | kwarg |  | Optional classification scheme the subject term belongs to (e.g. a subject-classification code list). |

</section>

<section #sh-author | `<author>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *author*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<author>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<author>`

<code #code:sh-author-1>
<author | Jane Goodall>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-author-1>
<author | Jane Goodall>
</minipage>

*Backward-compatible casual form (carried forward from before the structured-interface reconciliation). The pipe content sits as text content of &lt;author&gt;; it is NOT lifted to a &lt;name&gt; child — authors who want the structured shape use the kwarg form below or the child-tag form. JATS export reads the name string either way.*

<code #code:sh-author-2>
<author name="Jane Goodall" orcid="0000-0001-2345-6789" affiliation="Cambridge University" +corresponding />
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-author-2>
<author name="Jane Goodall" orcid="0000-0001-2345-6789" affiliation="Cambridge University" +corresponding />
</minipage>

*Kwarg form. Each lifted kwarg becomes a child tag at the gate; +corresponding stays as a boolean kwarg on the canonical Layer 1 &lt;author&gt;.*

<code #code:sh-author-3>
<author +corresponding>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <affiliation | Cambridge University>
  <orcid | 0000-0001-2345-6789>
  <email | jane@example.com>
</author>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-author-3>
<author +corresponding>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <affiliation | Cambridge University>
  <orcid | 0000-0001-2345-6789>
  <email | jane@example.com>
</author>
</minipage>

*Child-tag form. The canonical Layer 1 shape. Both this form and the equivalent kwarg form above produce the same Layer 1 output.*

<code #code:sh-author-4>
<meta>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <author | David Attenborough>
  <author +corresponding | Charles Darwin>
</meta>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-author-4>
<meta>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <author | David Attenborough>
  <author +corresponding | Charles Darwin>
</meta>
</minipage>

*Multiple authors are sibling &lt;author&gt; elements inside &lt;meta&gt;. The third is the corresponding author. JATS export groups them as &lt;contrib-group&gt;.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | The author's name. Authored as a kwarg, lifted at the normalize-to-canonical gate to a <name> child tag. Equivalent to authoring <name \| ...> inside the <author>'s child-tag form. |
| `affiliation` | kwarg |  | Author's institutional affiliation. Lifts to <affiliation>. |
| `orcid` | kwarg |  | Author's ORCID identifier (e.g., 0000-0000-0000-0000). Lifts to <orcid>. |
| `email` | kwarg |  | Author's contact email address. Lifts to <email>. |
| `corresponding` | kwarg | `true` `false` | Marks this author as the corresponding author (JATS contrib corresp="yes"). A scalar marker — stays as a kwarg/ attribute on the canonical Layer 1 <author>; never lifted to a child tag. Both surface forms are accepted: +corresponding (boolean shorthand) and corresponding=true (explicit kwarg) both normalize to a `corresponding="true"` attribute on the canonical Layer 1 node. The structured-element gate promotes the +form into the kwarg surface so the schema renderer's attribute mapping fires uniformly. |

</section>

<section #sh-editor | `<editor>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *editor*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<editor>`.

An editor of the document — a contributor, parallel to `<author>` — authored as a child of `<meta>`. It takes the same surface forms as `<author>`: a casual pipe name, a kwarg form, or the structured child-tag form. Repeat it for multiple editors.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<editor>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-editor-1>
<meta type=book>
  <title | A Natural History of Elephants>
  <editor | Jane Goodall>
  <editor name="David Attenborough" affiliation="BBC" />
</meta>
</code>

<code #code:sh-editor-2>
<editor>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <affiliation | Cambridge University>
  <orcid | 0000-0001-2345-6789>
</editor>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source. Like `<author>`, each lifted kwarg becomes a child tag at the gate; the child-tag form is the canonical Layer 1 shape. JATS export groups editors in the `<contrib-group>` with `contrib-type="editor"`.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | The editor's name. Lifts to a `<name>` child. |
| `affiliation` | kwarg |  | Institutional affiliation. Lifts to `<affiliation>`. |
| `orcid` | kwarg |  | ORCID identifier. Lifts to `<orcid>`. |
| `email` | kwarg |  | Contact email. Lifts to `<email>`. |
| `role` | kwarg |  | Optional editorial role (e.g. `series` for a series editor). |

</section>

<section #sh-date | `<date>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *date*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<date>`.

A date associated with the document, authored as a child of `<meta>`. Author the date as an ISO string in the content; the `type` kwarg says which date it is (publication, received, accepted, …).

Registers:

- Canonical: `<date>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-date-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <date | 2024-03-15>
  <date type=received | 2023-11-02>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `published` `received` `accepted` … | Which date this is. A publication date maps to JATS `<pub-date>`; other types map to `<date>` entries in the article history. Default is the publication date. |
| `format` | kwarg |  | Optional display-format hint for the rendered date. |

</section>

<section #sh-publication-date | `<publication-date>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *publication-date*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<publication-date>` (JATS `<pub-date>`).

The document's publication date, authored as a child of `<meta>`. It is the explicit, named form of the publication date (equivalent to `<date type=published>`); use it when you want the publication date to read unambiguously in the source.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<publication-date>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-publication-date-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <publication-date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `format` | kwarg |  | Optional display-format hint for the rendered date. |

</section>

<section #sh-license | `<license>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *license*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<license>`.

The document's license, authored as a child of `<meta>`. Author the human-readable license name as the content; the `href` kwarg carries the canonical license URL.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<license>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-license-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <license href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" | CC BY 4.0>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `href` | kwarg |  | The canonical URL of the license. Renders as a link on the license name. |

</section>

<section #sh-lang | `<lang>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *lang*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<lang>`.

The document's primary language, authored as a child of `<meta>`. Author a BCP-47 language tag (`en`, `en-GB`, `fr`) as the content. On the HTML surface it becomes the `xml:lang` / `lang` attribute of the document.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<lang>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-lang-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <lang | en>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

`<lang>` takes no attributes. Its content is the language tag.

</section>

<section #sh-version | `<version>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *version*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<version>`.

A version string for the document, authored as a child of `<meta>`. Useful for preprints and living documents that are revised over time.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<version>` (child of `<meta>`)

<code #code:sh-version-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <version | 2.1>
</meta>
</code>

*Front-matter — shown as source.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

`<version>` takes no attributes. Its content is the version string.

</section>
